<h2>Bookshelf</h2>

<p class="section-intro">Books that shaped how I think about software, work, and building things.</p>

<ul class="link-list">
  <li>
    <strong>A Philosophy of Software Design</strong> — John Ousterhout
    <span class="link-desc">— The best book on managing complexity in software. Short, opinionated, essential.</span>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>The Phoenix Project</strong> — Gene Kim, Kevin Behr, George Spafford
    <span class="link-desc">— A novel about IT, DevOps, and digging yourself out of operational hell. You'll recognise the characters.</span>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>A World Without Email</strong> — Cal Newport
    <span class="link-desc">— Why the hyperactive hive mind is killing knowledge work, and what to do about it.</span>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Slow Productivity</strong> — Cal Newport
    <span class="link-desc">— Do fewer things, work at a natural pace, obsess over quality. A manifesto against busyness.</span>
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>So Good They Can't Ignore You</strong> — Cal Newport
    <span class="link-desc">— Why "follow your passion" is bad advice. Build rare and valuable skills instead.</span>
  </li>
</ul>
